    {
      "@id": "https://w3id.org/squirrelpapers/v6/i4/e4#title",
      "@type": "crm:E35_Title",
      "crm:P190_has_symbolic_content": "Best Practices on Archaeological 3D Data Management: Methods and Approaches to Capture, Generate, and Disseminate 3D Information in Archaeology\""
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://w3id.org/squirrelpapers/v6/i4/e5",
      "@type": [
        "bibo:Document",
        "crm:E73_Information_Object",
        "dcat:Dataset",
        "fabio:Expression",
        "lrmoo:F2_Expression"
      ],
      "bibo:abstract": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Infrastructures for Research Data Management (RDM) and applying the FAIR (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, Re-usable) and Open Science Principles are key features in modern (digital) Cultural Heritage. Nowadays, computer applications, as well as statistical and computational approaches, constitute a big part of the toolbox of every CH researcher, as they open tremendous possibilities for all research. Several initiatives, such as the German National Research Data Infrastructure (NFDI) – especially NFDI4Objects – or the European Open Science Cloud (EOSC), use this topic to strengthen new technologies along the individual object biography. This includes (a) comprehending representations of physical objects as research data, (b) relating them to individual contexts, (c) transforming them adequately into the digital space, and (d) curating them according to domain-specific requirements. This leads to a paradigm derived from the FAIRification workflow: (i) capturing, (ii) semantic (meta) data modelling and qualifying, (iii) applying (scientific) analysis, (iv) sharing the data by visualisation and FAIRified publication, (v) interlinking and integrating data into knowledge graphs.\nTo support the activities within the before mentioned paradigm, this session invites contributions dealing with various aspects of RDM, FAIR and Open Science, but not limited to:\napplying new documentation methods for data capturing in the CH domain\napplying (semantic) data modelling (e.g. CIDOC CRM) for data qualification (e.g. RDF, LPG, etc.)\napplying new scientific analysis in Cultural Heritage, not limited to natural sciences, geosciences, geodesy, conservation sciences\nFAIRification Tools and Research Software (incl., e.g. small R/Python scripts, “CAA Little Minions”) and statistical/computational approaches\nconcepts to share research (meta) data in free and open available comprehensible FAIRified representations\nmethods and concepts for integrating and interlinking research data into domain-focused and overarching knowledge graphs\nworking together with Citizen Science and their knowledge bases/repositories (e.g. Wikiverse, Open Street Map, Portable Antiquities Scheme, …)\ndealing with legacy data, data sustainability and long-term archiving\npresenting experiences as DOs (Good Practices) and DON’Ts (Worst Practices)\napplying AI methods and tools for CH"
      },
      "bibo:authorList": {
        "@id": "https://w3id.org/squirrelpapers/v6/i4/e5#authors"
      },
      "bibo:doi": "10.5281/zenodo.10992401",
      "bibo:locator": "#5",
